<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of al-Qarawiyyin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/859">859</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as al-Qarawiyyin mosque, Qarawiyyin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarawiyyin-mosque">al-Qarawiyyin</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, began as a mosque founded in 859 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima-al-fihri">Fatima al-Fihri</a>. Rebuilt and enlarged under later dynasties, it became one of the leading centres of Islamic learning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, teaching law, theology, grammar and the sciences. It was integrated into Morocco's modern university system in 1963 and is often cited as the oldest continuously operating institution of higher learning.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/university-of-al-qarawiyyin.jpg" alt="University of al-Qarawiyyin" width="320" height="338" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abdel Hassouni · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:University_of_Al_Qaraouiyine.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the 9th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty</a>, the first Islamic state based in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. According to the Rawd al-Qirtas of Ibn Abi Zar, one of the main early sources, the mosque was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima-al-fihri">Fatima al-Fihri</a>, daughter of a wealthy merchant, Mohammed al-Fihri. Her family had migrated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> to Fez, which gave the mosque its name, and she vowed to spend her whole inheritance on a mosque suitable for her community. Her sister Mariam is reputed to have founded the al-Andalusiyyin Mosque in the same year.</p><p>It is unclear when the mosque began to act formally as an educational institution. Major mosques of the early Islamic period were multi-functional buildings where teaching took place alongside other activities. The earliest clear evidence of teaching, according to the historian Abdelhadi Tazi, dates from 1121.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Many scholars place its high point in the 13th and 14th centuries, when the curriculum was broadest. Subjects taught around then or soon after included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-jurisprudence">fiqh</a>, grammar, rhetoric, logic, medicine, mathematics, astronomy and geography, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alchemy">alchemy</a> was never officially taught as too unorthodox. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinid dynasty</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> around the mosque, the Saffarin Madrasa in 1271, al-Attarine in 1323 and the Mesbahiya in 1346. They housed students from other towns, many of them poor.</p><p>The institution declined with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> in later centuries and its curriculum narrowed to the traditional Islamic sciences and Arabic linguistics. In 1788–89 the Alawi sultan Muhammad ibn Abdallah regulated the programme but excluded logic, philosophy and the more radical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> texts. By the early 20th century its library had been reduced to around 1,600 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and 400 printed books.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> became a French protectorate in 1912, the French administration reformed its structure between 1914 and 1947 without modernising the content of teaching. Many Moroccan nationalists were educated there, and in 1937 the mosque was a rallying point for demonstrations. It was incorporated into the state university system in 1963 and renamed two years later. Women were first admitted in the 1940s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> and Guinness World Records have cited it as the oldest university or oldest continually operating higher-learning institution. Many scholars hold that the term university applies only to the institutions that took form in medieval Christian Europe, and they treat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarawiyyin-mosque">al-Qarawiyyin</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> until its reorganisation in 1963. Its alumni and teachers are said to include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maimonides">Maimonides</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-arabi">Ibn Arabi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-africanus">Leo Africanus</a>.</p><p>The mosque has grown over more than a thousand years under successive dynasties into the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, with a capacity of 22,000 worshippers. Its present teaching still concentrates on Islamic religious and legal sciences, Classical Arabic grammar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> law, delivered with students seated in a semi-circle around a sheikh.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “University of al-Qarawiyyin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Qarawiyyin</b> reached its apogee in prestige and scope, and the age produced fiqh, poetry, science, geography and above all history, in part because the dynasty wanted histories to legitimise it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marinid-sultanate">Marinid Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
